From daf8c2109b29799e7b95fafedee6f5c69fee9016 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hood Chatham <roberthoodchatham@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 16:18:22 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] Fix late-binding symbols with JSPI

Late-binding symbols get a JS stub import that resolves the symbol and
then makes an onward call. This breaks JSPI since JS frames cannot be
suspended through. (It also canonicalizes NaNs by round tripping them
through JS).

To fix this, when building with JSPI we generate a tiny wasm module per
import signature. The stub exported by this module makes a `call_indirect`
through a one-slot table. The slot initially holds an `$init` function that
calls out to JS to resolve the symbol; JS writes the resolved function into
the slot and returns, and `$init` then makes the onward call from wasm.

Unlike the earlier version of this change, this does not depend on
wasm-type-reflection: the signatures of the function imports are parsed
directly out of the type and import sections of the side module binary in
`getDylinkMetadata`. When `loadWebAssemblyModule` is given a pre-compiled
`WebAssembly.Module` instead of bytes we have no access to the signatures
and fall back to the JS stub (with a warning under ASSERTIONS).

The generated module only uses MVP wasm features (funcref table +
call_indirect), so there is no dependency on wasm GC either.

Fixes: #23395
